af(5,'bm81-operations-role','5.1','sl','The operations function','operations management','Managing the processes that transform inputs into goods or services / to deliver output meeting requirements for quality, quantity, timing and cost.','Explain two links between operations and other business functions at {name}.',[
('Szi Meals','Szi’s marketing team offers next-day meals, but the kitchen needs two days to source ingredients. Finance has restricted the stock budget and staff cannot add shifts without training.', ['Marketing promises must match operating capability.','A next-day offer conflicts with the two-day ingredient lead time.','Szi may lose customer trust unless the offer or sourcing process changes.'],['Finance affects operational inventory.','A restricted stock budget limits advance ingredient purchases.','Szi needs a costed stock plan rather than assuming the kitchen can meet every promoted order.']),
('Tzi Clinics','Tzi advertises evening diagnostic appointments. The equipment is available, but only two employees can operate it and HR has scheduled both on daytime shifts.', ['HR scheduling affects service output.','Equipment cannot create evening appointments without qualified operators.','Tzi needs trained cover or altered shifts to deliver the advertised service.'],['Marketing must reflect actual capacity.','Evening promotion can create bookings the clinic cannot fulfil.','Operations and marketing should agree available slots before advertising.']),
('Uzi Furniture','Uzi’s sales staff accept many custom finishes. Production must clean equipment between colours, while finance expects low unit costs based on long runs.', ['Marketing variety changes operational efficiency.','Custom finishes require more cleaning and shorter runs.','Uzi must price or schedule variety to avoid unexpected delays.'],['Finance assumptions depend on the production mix.','Long-run unit-cost estimates may understate changeover costs.','Operations must supply realistic run information for finance to forecast profit.']),
('Vzi Apps','Vzi sells a subscription app. Marketing plans a large launch, but operations expects server limits and customer-support staff have not learned the new features.', ['Marketing scale affects operating capacity.','A large launch could exceed server limits.','Vzi must test or expand infrastructure before attracting more simultaneous users.'],['HR training affects service quality.','Support staff cannot answer questions about unfamiliar features.','Training must precede launch if Vzi wants timely and accurate customer support.'])])
ef(5,'bm82-job-batch','5.2','sl','Job and batch production','job production','Producing a distinct one-off item or completing a specific customer task / usually using flexible resources and skills rather than a continuous standardized flow.','switching the proposed work from job to batch production',[
('Wzi Furniture','Wzi makes one-off dining tables. A retailer requests repeated batches of 40 identical tables. Existing private clients pay a premium for custom dimensions.','Repeated batches can reduce setup cost per table;Wzi can reuse the same design and settings for 40 units','Standardized batches may displace premium work;the workshop could lose profitable custom clients if capacity is fully used','Accept a separate batch schedule if contribution and capacity justify it; preserve custom slots where they create more value.','The retailer’s margin and the opportunity cost of lost custom work are unknown.'),
('Xzi Ceramics','Xzi produces individually designed mugs. A hotel orders 600 identical mugs in monthly batches, but kiln space is limited and failures vary by glaze.','Batching identical mugs can improve consistency and efficiency;shared glaze and firing settings reduce repeated preparation','A large failed batch can concentrate risk;limited kiln space also delays Xzi’s individual commissions','Trial a small hotel batch and verify yield before committing the kiln; retain a realistic buffer for commissions.','The failure rate for the hotel glaze and exact delivery schedule are unknown.'),
('Yzi Prints','Yzi prints custom art books. A school wants termly batches of identical workbooks at a lower unit price, while custom clients accept longer lead times.','Batch production can spread setup cost;identical workbooks need fewer layout and machine changes','Lower prices may not cover the full opportunity cost;termly peaks could occupy Yzi’s machines when custom orders arrive','Use spare periods for workbooks if margins cover incremental costs and promised delivery; price peak periods differently if needed.','Forecast custom demand and school order certainty are not given.'),
('Zzi Foods','Zzi prepares unique event cakes. A café wants batches of identical pastries every morning. The same ovens and decorators would serve both activities.','Batches can create regular predictable output;repeat pastries can smooth Zzi’s otherwise uneven event demand','Early daily batches may compete for skilled time and ovens;custom cakes could be delayed despite their premium price','Pilot pastries with a dedicated production window and compare incremental profit with displaced event capacity.','Morning oven availability and the café’s guaranteed order volume are uncertain.')])
ef(5,'bm83-flow-customization','5.2','sl','Mass production and mass customization','mass customization','Using production systems that combine high-volume efficiency with individual choice / often by assembling standardized modules into customer-selected configurations.','introducing the proposed mass-customization system',[
('Azo Cycles','Azo plans standard bicycle frames with customer-selected gears and handlebars. It currently produces one standard model in long runs. Retailers promise short delivery times.','Modular choice can improve customer fit;Azo can offer variety without redesigning each complete bicycle','Component coordination becomes more complex;missing one chosen gear set can delay an otherwise available bicycle','Use a limited modular range with reliable parts planning before expanding choices; avoid promises beyond assembly capability.','Demand by configuration and component lead times are unknown.'),
('Bzo Shoes','Bzo proposes standard sole units with customized upper colours. Customers can choose online, but colour demand is unpredictable and the minimum fabric order is large.','Customization may support differentiation;buyers can select colours while Bzo retains standard sole efficiency','Variety creates inventory risk;large fabric minimums may leave slow colours unused','Start with a narrow tested colour range and staged orders; expand only where demand supports minimum quantities.','The colour mix and supplier flexibility are not established.'),
('Czo Desks','Czo will offer modular desk sizes and storage combinations using standard parts. Its current flow line is efficient, but assembly errors rise when workers face many options.','Standard modules retain scale benefits;customers gain useful combinations without full bespoke manufacture','Complexity can increase errors and training needs;wrong modules may require costly reassembly or returns','Introduce clear configuration controls and training with a limited menu before promising unrestricted choice.','The cost of error prevention and customers’ willingness to pay for options are unknown.'),
('Dzo Meals','Dzo proposes personalized meal boxes assembled from standard cooked components. Customers can exclude ingredients, and safe separation is essential for some orders.','Modular assembly can meet different preferences;standard components avoid cooking every meal separately','Customization raises process-control risk;incorrect components can breach important dietary requirements','Proceed with a controlled menu and verified separation and checking processes; refuse options that cannot be delivered safely.','The error rate under trial conditions and additional checking time are unknown.')])
af(5,'bm84-location','5.4','sl','Location decisions','location decision','The choice of where to carry out a business activity / considering factors such as customers, labour, inputs, infrastructure, costs and external conditions.','Explain one advantage and one limitation for {name} of choosing the proposed new location.',[
('Ezo Foods', 'Ezo considers moving its bakery to a cheaper industrial site. Rent would fall, but the new site is farther from morning customers and public transport for staff is poor.', ['Lower rent can reduce operating cost.', 'the industrial site may release funds for equipment.', 'A lower regular rent can reduce the sales needed to cover bakery overhead.'], ['Access costs and lost demand may outweigh rent savings.', 'customers and workers would find the bakery harder to reach.', 'Lost morning sales and recruitment difficulties could reduce operating profit despite cheaper rent.']),
('Fzo Optics', 'Fzo considers a factory near specialist suppliers and a technical college. The site has higher rent and occasional electricity interruptions.', ['Supplier and skills proximity can improve capability.', 'Fzo may recruit trained technicians and receive inputs faster.', 'Shorter input lead times and less initial training can reduce production delays.'], ['Unreliable power can disrupt precision work.', 'higher rent still applies during downtime.', 'Interrupted precision work may create defective lenses and missed delivery commitments.']),
('Gzo Data', 'Gzo considers a rural data centre with cheap land and renewable electricity. Fibre access requires a new connection and clients require high uptime.', ['Land and energy conditions may reduce long-term cost.', 'renewable supply can support Gzo’s environmental position.', 'Lower ongoing energy costs could reduce the cost of hosting each client.'], ['Connectivity risk is critical to service.', 'cheap land is of little value if the fibre link cannot meet uptime requirements.', 'Connection failure could breach client service commitments and cause lost contracts.']),
('Hzo Furniture', 'Hzo considers a site near a timber cluster. Skilled workers and suppliers are nearby, but local housing costs make wage expectations higher.', ['Cluster access can reduce training and sourcing difficulties.', 'experienced workers and suppliers are close to Hzo.', 'Existing specialist skills can help the workshop begin reliable production sooner.'], ['Higher labour costs may offset supply benefits.', 'housing pressure can raise wages and staff turnover.', 'The higher wage bill may offset the savings from nearby timber suppliers.'])],exam='paper-2',scope='Current guide: this content is AO2; two applied explanation chains, no AO3 evaluation demanded.')
ef(5,'bm85-outsourcing','5.4','sl','Outsourcing','outsourcing','Using another organization to perform an activity or provide an input / rather than carrying it out entirely within the business itself.','outsourcing the proposed activity',[
('Izo Clinics','Izo proposes outsourcing appointment booking. A specialist offers lower processing costs, but callers often ask sensitive questions and need rapid escalation to clinical staff.','Specialist scale can reduce routine administration cost;Izo’s staff may focus more on clinical support','Coordination and confidentiality risks increase;misdirected sensitive queries could delay care and damage trust','Outsource only routine booking with secure records and tested escalation; keep sensitive clinical communication within a qualified team.','Vendor accuracy, data controls and the cost of failed handovers are unknown.'),
('Jzo Wear','Jzo proposes outsourcing stitching to a lower-cost supplier. Its brand depends on repairability, while the supplier uses seams difficult to unpick.','External production may lower direct manufacturing cost;Jzo avoids expanding its own stitching capacity','The supplier’s process may undermine differentiation;unrepairable seams conflict with the promise supporting Jzo’s premium price','Require verifiable repairable specifications and audit trial output before outsourcing; reject a saving that destroys the brand benefit.','The full quality cost and supplier’s ability to meet repair standards are unproven.'),
('Kzo Apps','Kzo proposes outsourcing customer support to a vendor in another time zone. The vendor offers night coverage but staff know little about Kzo’s complex software.','Coverage can improve availability;customers can contact support when Kzo’s own daytime team is offline','Product knowledge may be weak;poor answers could increase repeat contacts and frustration','Use a trained first-line service with fast expert escalation and monitored resolution quality; do not judge solely on cost per call.','Training time and the proportion of complex queries are unknown.'),
('Lzo Foods','Lzo proposes outsourced chilled delivery. The vendor has a large network, but its contract permits shared loads and offers limited compensation for temperature failures.','A wider network can improve reach;Lzo can serve new areas without owning more refrigerated vehicles','Cold-chain control is partly surrendered;shared loads and limited compensation expose Lzo’s food quality and reputation','Proceed only with temperature evidence, enforceable standards and adequate remedies; retain own delivery for critical routes if needed.','The vendor’s failure record and full loss exposure are not supplied.')])
ef(5,'bm86-reshoring','5.4','sl','Offshoring and reshoring','reshoring','Bringing a previously offshored business activity back to the home country / whether operated internally or through a domestic supplier.','reshoring the proposed production activity',[
('Mzo Tools','Mzo offshored casting five years ago. Overseas unit costs remain lower, but deliveries now take ten weeks and demand changes quickly. A domestic supplier can deliver in two weeks.','Shorter lead times improve responsiveness;Mzo can adjust tool orders with less stock held during uncertain demand','Domestic unit costs are higher;the lead-time saving must offset lower production margins','Reshore volatile or urgent products first if total inventory and service gains justify the premium; retain stable lines offshore where appropriate.','The cost of inventory, missed sales and supplier transition is unknown.'),
('Nzo Toys','Nzo makes toys overseas. Quality faults are discovered after long shipments, while a home-country plant would allow closer inspections but requires skilled staff who are scarce.','Closer production can improve communication and quality response;faults can be corrected before a long international shipment','Skill scarcity may limit output and raise cost;a domestic plant may not immediately achieve the expected quality or volume','Use a staged return with trained staff and trial output; do not assume geographic proximity alone solves quality.','The availability of skilled workers and comparative defect rates are unverified.'),
('Ozo Fabrics','Ozo considers bringing dyeing home. Customers increasingly request traceable short runs, but the home location requires investment in wastewater treatment.','Closer short-run production can support traceability and flexibility;Ozo can respond to changing customer designs','Environmental capital costs are substantial;water treatment may make the return uneconomic without a price premium','Reshore if verified demand for traceable short runs covers compliant total cost; price the environmental investment explicitly.','Customers’ willingness to pay and treatment operating costs are unknown.'),
('Pzo Devices','Pzo considers reshoring final assembly while keeping component production overseas. Its main objective is faster delivery, but imported components account for most of the current lead time.','Domestic assembly can shorten the final stage and improve coordination;Pzo can customize products closer to customers','The main bottleneck may remain;overseas component delays could prevent the promised faster delivery','Reshore only after mapping the full supply chain and securing critical components; a partial return may yield limited timing gains.','The proportion of delay at each supply-chain stage is not supplied.')])
for name,product,p,v,f,q in [('Qzo Cups','cups',18,10,32000,6000),('Rzo Meals','meal boxes',25,15,45000,6500),('Szo Kits','learning kits',40,24,64000,5500),('Tzo Bags','bags',55,33,88000,6000)]:
    c=p-v;be=f/c;profit=q*c-f;mos=q-be
    items=[calc('a','Calculate contribution per unit.',f'Price−variable cost={p}−{v}.',f'USD {c:.2f} per unit.'),calc('b','Calculate break-even output.',f'Fixed cost/contribution per unit={f}/{c}.',f'{be:,.0f} units per month.'),calc('c','Calculate monthly profit at forecast sales.',f'{q}×{c}−{f}.',f'USD {profit:,.2f}.'),explain('d','Explain one usefulness and one limitation of this break-even calculation.',['It identifies the volume needed to cover the stated costs.',f'Forecast sales exceed break-even by {mos:,.0f} units, providing a numerical margin of safety.','The calculation assumes constant unit price and variable cost within the relevant range.','Discounts, input-price changes or unsold output could make actual profit differ from the forecast.'])]
    add(5,'bm87-break-even','5.5','sl',f'{name}: break-even and profit','Break-even analysis',f'{name} sells {product} for USD {p} per unit. Variable cost is USD {v} per unit and monthly fixed cost USD {f:,}. Forecast monthly sales and output are both {q:,} units. Assume one product, no inventory change, sufficient capacity and constant price and variable cost in this range. Show workings; money to two decimals and quantities as whole units.',items,kind='data-response',check=f'Contribution={c};BE={be};profit={profit};MOS={mos}; revenue minus variable andfixedcost independentlyequivalent.')
for name,product,p,v,f,target,q in [('Uzo Frames','frames',35,19,48000,24000,5000),('Vzo Tiles','tiles',12,7,25000,15000,9000),('Wzo Lamps','lamps',60,36,72000,36000,6000),('Xzo Boxes','boxes',8,5,18000,12000,12000)]:
    out=(f+target)/(p-v);price=v+(f+target)/q
    items=[calc('a','Calculate the minimum whole-unit sales needed to achieve the target profit at the current price.',f'({f}+{target})/({p}−{v}).',f'{math.ceil(out):,} units per month; round upward if fractional.'),calc('b',f'Calculate the theoretical selling price needed for the target profit at {q:,} units, to two decimals. Also state the minimum price in whole cents that would meet or exceed the target.',f'{v}+({f}+{target})/{q}.',f'Theoretical price USD {price:.2f} per unit; minimum whole-cent price USD {math.ceil(round(price*100,10))/100:.2f} per unit. The accuracy mark requires both, including upward cent rounding where needed.'),explain('c','Explain two reasons the calculated target price may not achieve the target profit in practice.',['The calculation assumes the planned quantity can be sold at that price.','Customers may reduce purchases when price changes.','A demand estimate is required before treating the target output as assured.','The cost assumptions may change at the planned volume.','Capacity expansion or supplier price changes can alter fixed or variable cost.','The calculated price must be revised if the underlying costs differ from the forecast.'])]
    add(5,'bm88-target-profit','5.5','sl',f'{name}: target output and price','Target profit analysis',f'{name} sells {product}. Current price is USD {p}; variable cost USD {v} per unit; monthly fixed cost USD {f:,}. Management targets monthly profit USD {target:,}. For part b assume sales of {q:,} units and unchanged costs. Ignore taxes and inventory. Show workings; quantities are whole units and prices to two decimals.',items,kind='data-response',check=f'Targetoutputunrounded={out};minimum={math.ceil(out)};targetprice={price}; substitutingprice*quantity−v*quantity−fixed recovers target.')
for name,product,p,v,f,newf,newv,q in [('Yzo Foods','food packs',20,12,40000,64000,8,7000),('Zzo Prints','prints',30,18,72000,108000,12,8000),('Azu Parts','parts',45,27,90000,144000,18,9000),('Bzu Wear','garments',50,32,108000,162000,23,10000)]:
    oldbe=f/(p-v);newbe=newf/(p-newv);oldprofit=q*(p-v)-f;newprofit=q*(p-newv)-newf
    xmax=math.ceil(q*1.25); ymax=p*xmax;xx=lambda x:70+x/xmax*600;yy=lambda y:280-y/ymax*220
    body='<path d="M70 280 L700 280 M70 280 L70 35" fill="none" stroke="#172554"/>'+svg_text(280,320,'Monthly output / sales, units')+svg_text(10,25,'USD per month',13)
    for y0,slope,color,label in [(0,p,'#15803d','Total revenue'),(f,v,'#2563eb','Current total cost'),(newf,newv,'#b45309','Proposed total cost')]:
        body+=f'<path d="M{xx(0)} {yy(y0):.2f} L{xx(xmax):.2f} {yy(y0+slope*xmax):.2f}" fill="none" stroke="{color}" stroke-width="2"/>'
        body+=svg_text(450,48+[(0,p),(f,v),(newf,newv)].index((y0,slope))*22,label,13)
    items=[calc('a','Calculate current and proposed theoretical break-even output, to two decimal places.',f'Current={f}/({p}−{v}); proposed={newf}/({p}−{newv}).',f'Current {oldbe:,.2f} units; proposed {newbe:,.2f} units. Method mark for both setups; accuracy mark for both. If minimum whole-unit output is also stated, round upward.'),calc('b',f'Calculate the change in monthly profit at {q:,} units.',f'Proposed [{q}×({p}−{newv})−{newf}] minus current [{q}×({p}−{v})−{f}].',f'USD {newprofit-oldprofit:,.2f} increase.'),ten('c',f'Recommend whether {name} should adopt the proposed automated process.',name,'the automated process',f'At {q:,} units monthly profit increases from USD {oldprofit:,} to USD {newprofit:,}',f'Higher fixed cost increases commitment and demand risk; the saving depends on retaining the stated variable cost and sufficient sales','Adopt if credible demand, implementation finance and quality support the forecast; test a lower-volume scenario before replacing the current process.','The diagram omits financing, transition disruption, quality changes and uncertainty in future demand.')]
    add(5,'bm89-automation-break-even','5.5','sl',f'{name}: changing the cost structure','Break-even changes',f'{name} makes {product}. Unit price stays at USD {p}. Current monthly fixed cost is USD {f:,}, variable cost USD {v} per unit. Automation would change fixed cost to USD {newf:,} and variable cost to USD {newv} per unit. At the forecast, sales and output are both {q:,} units monthly, with no inventory. The chart is schematic; use the stated data for calculations. Ignore finance charges in the numerical comparison. Show workings.',items,kind='data-response',svg=svgwrap(body,760,350),check=f'BEcurrent={oldbe};BEproposed={newbe};profitcurrent={oldprofit};profitproposed={newprofit}; incrementalprofit={newprofit-oldprofit}. Allcomparisonunitsmonth.')
ef(5,'bm90-circular-operations','5.2','sl','Circular operations and production methods','product life extension','A circular business approach that keeps products in use for longer / through activities such as repair, refurbishment, upgrading or resale.','introducing the proposed refurbishment operation',[
('Czu Furniture','Czu plans to refurbish returned desks in a dedicated job-production workshop. Its new-desk flow line is efficient, but returned desks vary in damage and dimensions.','Flexible job work suits varied returns;technicians can diagnose and repair each desk while recovering product value','Refurbishment is harder to standardize;inspection and skilled labour may make some returns uneconomic','Use separate flexible refurbishment with clear acceptance criteria; do not force irregular returns through the new-desk flow line.','The return-condition mix and achievable resale prices are unknown.'),
('Dzu Phones','Dzu proposes batch refurbishment of used phones by model. Battery condition varies, and secure data removal is essential before resale.','Grouping models can improve efficiency;shared tools and parts simplify repeated refurbishment tasks','Variation and data risk remain;unreliable batteries or incomplete erasure can damage customer trust','Batch only after individual inspection and verified data removal; track failures and warranty cost before expansion.','Expected battery replacement rates and warranty returns are uncertain.'),
('Ezu Tools','Ezu proposes repairing rental tools between uses. Repairs range from quick checks to complete motor replacement, and customers expect safe equipment at collection.','Life extension can reduce new purchases;repairing serviceable tools can recover value and support availability','Repair bottlenecks can disrupt service;variable tasks make turnaround difficult to promise','Separate rapid inspection from complex repairs and maintain a safe spare pool; retire tools where repair is uneconomic or unsafe.','The distribution of repair times and safe remaining life is unknown.'),
('Fzu Textiles','Fzu plans to convert returned garments into standardized fabric panels. Sorting by fibre is labour-intensive and mixed materials cannot all be processed in the same way.','Recovery can create a useful material stream;standard panels may convert waste into saleable inputs','Sorting complexity raises cost and limits yield;mixed fibres may not meet the required panel specification','Pilot selected compatible fibres and verify yield before scaling; disclose the actual recovered content.','No data establish collection volumes, fibre composition or panel demand.')])
ef(5,'bm91-lean-jit','5.3','ahl','Lean production and JIT','just-in-time production','Organizing inputs and output to arrive or be produced when required / with minimal inventory rather than holding large stocks throughout the process.','introducing the proposed JIT system',[
('Gzu Foods','Gzu wants daily fresh ingredient deliveries to reduce waste. Its sole supplier is across a mountain road that closes unpredictably, and the kitchen cannot substitute key ingredients.','Lower stock can reduce spoilage and storage;daily fresh deliveries match Gzu’s short ingredient life','Supply disruption can stop output;road closures and no substitute leave Gzu unable to fulfil meals','Use selective JIT for reliable inputs with buffers or backup sourcing for critical ingredients; do not eliminate all resilience.','Road-closure frequency and the cost of a backup source are unknown.'),
('Hzu Motors','Hzu proposes frequent small component deliveries. A supplier offers reliable daily slots but requires electronic order forecasts; Hzu’s sales schedule changes without notice.','Smaller stocks can release working capital;components need not wait long in Hzu’s warehouse','Unstable information undermines JIT;unannounced schedule changes may cause shortages despite supplier reliability','Improve shared forecasting and schedule discipline before reducing stocks; pilot stable components first.','The size and frequency of schedule changes are unmeasured.'),
('Izu Rooms','Izu applies lean ideas to hotel linen, ordering daily laundry returns rather than holding spares. A single machine breakdown at the laundry previously delayed deliveries.','Lower linen inventory reduces holding space and tied-up cash;daily returns can match expected occupancy','No buffer creates service risk;a laundry failure could leave occupied rooms without clean linen','Keep a costed safety stock for essential linen while improving supplier reliability and delivery information.','The expected cost of a service failure and minimum safe buffer are unknown.'),
('Jzu Prints','Jzu plans JIT paper purchasing to reduce warehouse costs. Its premium paper supplier has a four-week lead time and clients sometimes request urgent jobs.','Inventory reduction can reduce storage and finance cost;less paper sits unused in Jzu’s warehouse','Long lead times conflict with urgent demand;without stock Jzu may reject premium jobs','Retain buffer stock for long-lead premium paper and use JIT only for easily replenished grades.','Urgent demand frequency and inventory-financing savings are not provided.')])
ef(5,'bm92-quality','5.3','ahl','Quality assurance and TQM','quality assurance','Designing and managing processes to prevent quality failures / rather than relying only on inspecting finished output and rejecting defects.','replacing reliance on final inspection with the proposed quality-assurance approach',[
('Kzu Glass','Kzu rejects 8% of finished glassware. Managers propose supplier standards, operator checks and quality circles. Employees fear that reporting problems will lead to blame.','Prevention can reduce waste before completion;operator and supplier checks may catch causes before glass is finished','A blame culture can undermine reporting;staff may hide faults instead of using the quality circles','Train teams and create a learning culture while retaining essential final checks; measure causes and improvement rather than punish reporting.','The sources of the 8% rejection rate are not identified.'),
('Lzu Meals','Lzu checks meals only after packing. It proposes documented cooking controls and staff training, but the owner wants to remove final checks immediately to save cost.','Process control can prevent unsafe or inconsistent output;trained staff can address variation before packing','Removing checks prematurely creates risk;new procedures may not yet be reliable','Validate the process and retain proportionate checks during transition; use evidence before reducing inspection.','No trial demonstrates that the new controls achieve the required quality.'),
('Mzu Clinics','Mzu proposes TQM across appointment, testing and results communication. Current departments meet separate targets, and each blames the others for delayed patient results.','Whole-organization quality can improve coordination;shared patient outcomes connect departments rather than reward isolated speed','Implementation requires time and management commitment;departments may defend existing targets and resist joint accountability','Align measures, responsibilities and improvement meetings around complete patient journeys before expecting quick savings.','The contribution of each stage to total delay is unknown.'),
('Nzu Devices','Nzu proposes benchmarking against a low-defect rival and seeking a recognized quality standard. Its manager assumes certification will guarantee zero customer complaints.','Benchmarking and standards can identify process gaps;Nzu can adopt consistent controls rather than ad hoc inspection','Certification does not guarantee perfect outcomes;formal compliance may not address every customer need or changing risk','Use standards as a framework with ongoing defect and customer-feedback review; avoid treating the certificate as a substitute for improvement.','The rival’s process comparability and customers’ main complaint causes are unknown.')])
for name,inputname,use,lead,buffer,order,extra in [('Ozu Foods','ingredient packs',240,3,360,2400,2),('Pzu Tools','steel blanks',180,4,420,1800,3),('Qzu Wear','fabric rolls',35,6,140,700,2),('Rzu Kits','sensor modules',120,5,300,1500,4)]:
    reorder=use*lead+buffer;remaining=reorder-use*lead;delayremain=buffer-use*extra
    body='<path d="M70 250 L700 250 M70 250 L70 25" fill="none" stroke="#172554"/>'+svg_text(280,292,'Time, days')+svg_text(10,20,'Stock, units',13)
    maxs=buffer+order;scale=200/maxs
    # Neutral chart marks the given levels; it does not label the requested reorder level.
    body+=f'<path d="M70 {250-maxs*scale:.2f} L450 {250-buffer*scale:.2f} L450 {250-maxs*scale:.2f} L690 {250-(maxs-use*(order/use*0.6))*scale:.2f}" fill="none" stroke="#2563eb" stroke-width="2"/>'+svg_text(75,40,f'Maximum after normal receipt: {maxs} units',13)+svg_text(75,235,f'Buffer: {buffer} units',13)
    items=[calc('a','Calculate the reorder level.',f'Daily use×lead time+buffer={use}×{lead}+{buffer}.',f'{reorder:,} units.'),calc('b',f'Calculate the physical stock remaining immediately before a delivery that is {extra} days later than the normal lead time is received. If stock is exhausted, also state the unmet demand before receipt.',f'Buffer−extra daily use={buffer}−{use}×{extra}.',f'{max(0,delayremain):,} units of stock'+(f'; unmet demand {abs(delayremain):,} units.' if delayremain<0 else '; no stockout on these assumptions.')),explain('c','Explain one advantage and one disadvantage of holding the stated buffer.',['Buffer stock protects against a limited delivery delay.','It can allow production to continue after the normal receipt date.','The buffer ties up working capital and storage capacity.','If use is lower than forecast or the input deteriorates, the held stock can create additional loss.'])]
    add(5,'bm93-stock-control','5.6','ahl',f'{name}: stock control and delay','Production planning and inventory',f'{name} uses {use} {inputname} every day at a constant rate. Normal supplier lead time is {lead} days; buffer stock is {buffer} units; each replenishment order is {order} units. Orders are placed as stock reaches the reorder level and arrive at the end of the normal lead time. Assume no demand change, no overlap of orders and no alternative supply. The chart is schematic. Show workings and use whole units.',items,kind='data-response',svg=svgwrap(body,760,320),check=f'Reorder={reorder};normalreceiptstock={remaining}=buffer; afterextradelay={delayremain}; stockfloored0/unmetdemandreported ifnegative. Orderquantity not confused withreorderlevel.')

# Four production-metric investigations: the metric, denominator and time basis differ.
items=[calc('a','Calculate monthly capacity utilization.', '7200/9600×100.', '75.00%.'),calc('b','Calculate labour productivity per worker per month.','7200/24.','300 units per worker per month.'),explain('c','Explain one benefit and one risk of increasing utilization without adding workers.',['Higher output can spread fixed factory cost over more units.','Using existing spare capacity may lower average fixed cost.','Additional workload may cause fatigue or rushed maintenance.','If quality or machine reliability deteriorates, extra output may not create a sustainable gain.'])]
add(5,'bm94-production-metrics','5.6','ahl','Szu Panels: capacity and labour','Production planning metrics','Szu Panels produces 7200 panels in one month. The maximum practical output with its current equipment and normal working arrangements is 9600 panels monthly. It employs 24 production workers throughout the month. Use output per worker, not per hour, for labour productivity. Show workings; percentages to two decimals.',items,kind='data-response',check='7200/9600=0.75;7200/24=300units/worker/month. Practicalcapacity distinguished from actualoutput.')
items=[calc('a','Calculate output per machine-hour.','18000/600.','30 units per machine-hour.'),calc('b','Calculate the defect rate.','540/18000×100.','3.00%.'),explain('c','Explain why rising gross output per machine-hour does not necessarily mean higher usable productivity.',['Gross output includes defective units.','More units may be produced quickly while a higher share fails inspection.','Usable output per machine-hour would deduct rejected units.','The business should examine quality-adjusted output and rework cost before judging the productivity gain.'])]
add(5,'bm94-production-metrics','5.6','ahl','Tzu Moulding: capital productivity and defects','Production planning metrics','Tzu Moulding produces 18000 units during a month using 600 machine-hours. Of these units, 540 fail inspection and cannot be sold without rework. Define capital productivity for this question as gross units per machine-hour. Show workings; percentages to two decimals.',items,kind='data-response',check='18000/600=30grossunits/machinehour;540/18000*100=3%;usable=17460;qualityadjusted=29.1units/machinehour.')
items=[calc('a','Calculate monthly operating profit before interest and tax.','Contribution60000−fixedoperatingcost40000.','USD 20,000.00.'),calc('b','Calculate degree of operating leverage using contribution divided by operating profit.','60000/20000.','3.00.'),explain('c','Explain one implication and one limitation of the operating-leverage result.',['A degree of 3 implies profit is sensitive to sales volume around this point.','Under constant price, unit variable cost and fixed cost, a 1% volume increase implies approximately a 3% profit increase.','The relationship depends on the assumed cost range.','Capacity changes, price discounts or different product mixes can invalidate the simple proportional prediction.'])]
add(5,'bm94-production-metrics','5.6','ahl','Uzu Prints: operating leverage','Production planning metrics','Uzu Prints has monthly total contribution of USD 60000 and fixed operating costs of USD 40000. There are no other operating costs. For this question, degree of operating leverage equals total contribution divided by operating profit before interest and tax. Assume price, unit variable cost and fixed cost remain unchanged near current volume. Show workings.',items,kind='data-response',check='PBIT=60000−40000=20000;DOL=3;at+1%volume contribution60600,PBIT20600=>+3%.')
items=[calc('a','Calculate labour productivity before and after the change.','Before: 4800/20; after: 5400/18.','Before: 240; after: 300 units per worker per month. Method mark for both setups; accuracy mark for both.'),calc('b','Calculate the percentage change in labour productivity.','(300−240)/240×100.','25.00% increase.'),explain('c','Explain why these results alone do not establish that the change benefited all stakeholders.',['Higher output per worker may improve cost competitiveness.','The business produces more units with fewer workers.','Employees may face job losses or greater work intensity.','Quality, pay, safety and customer outcomes are needed before judging the wider effect.'])]
add(5,'bm94-production-metrics','5.6','ahl','Vzu Foods: productivity change','Production planning metrics','Vzu Foods previously made 4800 units monthly with 20 workers. After reorganization it makes 5400 units with 18 workers. Both months have the same working days and product specification. Measure labour productivity as units per worker per month, without assuming hours worked are unchanged. Show workings; percentage to two decimals.',items,kind='data-response',check='Before240units/worker;after300;change25%;perworker not perhour, so no unprovidedhours assumption.')
for name,component,fixed,var,buy,q in [('Wzu Bikes','frames',36000,28,40,4000),('Xzu Lamps','bases',24000,12,18,5000),('Yzu Kits','cases',18000,8,14,3500),('Zzu Devices','boards',60000,35,50,5000)]:
    make=fixed+var*q;purchase=buy*q;cross=fixed/(buy-var)
    items=[calc('a','Calculate total cost to make and total cost to buy at the stated quantity.',f'CTM={fixed}+{var}×{q};CTB={buy}×{q}.',f'Make USD {make:,.2f}; buy USD {purchase:,.2f}. Method mark for both setups; accuracy mark for both.'),calc('b','Calculate the quantity at which total make and buy costs are equal.',f'{fixed}/({buy}−{var}).',f'{cross:,.0f} components.'),ten('c',f'Recommend whether {name} should make or buy the components.',name,'the make-or-buy decision',f'At {q:,} units, making costs USD {make:,} compared with buying at USD {purchase:,}',f'The numerical comparison excludes quality, reliability and future demand; making commits fixed cost while buying creates supplier dependence',f'On stated relevant costs {"make" if make<purchase else "buy"} is cheaper; choose only after verifying standards and demand around the {cross:,.0f}-unit crossover.','The supplier’s reliability, capacity constraints and future volume are not given.')]
    add(5,'bm95-make-buy','5.6','ahl',f'{name}: make or buy','Production planning and make/buy',f'{name} needs {q:,} {component} for one year. Making requires avoidable annual fixed cost USD {fixed:,} plus USD {var} per component. Buying costs USD {buy} per component, including delivery. All listed fixed cost is avoided if buying; there are no existing unavoidable overhead allocations, capacity constraints or other relevant monetary costs in the calculation. Show workings and give money to two decimals.',items,kind='data-response',check=f'Make={make};buy={purchase};cross={cross};crosscost={fixed+var*cross}={buy*cross}. Fixedcost expresslyavoidable; no sunkoverhead included.')
for name,project,durations in [('Aza Events','a venue launch',(4,3,5,2,2)),('Bza Foods','a new line',(3,5,4,6,2)),('Cza Wear','a product launch',(6,4,3,2,4)),('Dza Tools','a facility move',(2,4,5,5,3))]:
    a,b,c,d,e=durations;left=a+c;right=b+d;join=max(left,right);finish=join+e;critical='A–C–E' if left>right else 'B–D–E';short='B' if left>right else 'A';sd=b if short=='B' else a;slack=abs(left-right)
    nodes={1:(60,180),2:(265,65),3:(265,295),4:(500,180),5:(700,180)}
    edges=[(1,2,'A',a),(1,3,'B',b),(2,4,'C',c),(3,4,'D',d),(4,5,'E',e)]
    body=''
    for n1,n2,label,dur in edges:
        x1,y1=nodes[n1];x2,y2=nodes[n2];body+=f'<path d="M{x1} {y1} L{x2} {y2}" fill="none" stroke="#172554"/>'+svg_text((x1+x2)/2+8,(y1+y2)/2-9,f'{label}: {dur} days',14)
    for n,(x,y) in nodes.items():body+=f'<circle cx="{x}" cy="{y}" r="23" fill="#e0f2fe" stroke="#172554"/>'+svg_text(x-5,y+5,n)
    est={1:0,2:a,3:b,4:join,5:finish};lft={1:0,2:join-c,3:join-d,4:join,5:finish}
    sol=body+''.join(svg_text(x-35,25 if n==2 else y+45,f'EST {est[n]} / LFT {lft[n]}',12) for n,(x,y) in nodes.items())
    items=[calc('a','Identify the critical path and calculate the minimum project duration.',f'Compare A+C+E={left+e} with B+D+E={right+e}.',f'Critical path {critical}; duration {finish} days.'),({'label':'b','prompt':f'Calculate the total float and free float of activity {short}. Show both calculations.','marks':4},group('b',[step(f'Total float=latest finish event−earliest start event−duration={sd+slack}−0−{sd}.','M1'),step(f'Total float={slack} days.','A1'),step(f'Free float=earliest finish event−earliest start event−duration={sd}−0−{sd}.','M1'),step('Free float=0 days.','A1')])),explain('c','Explain two limitations of treating this minimum duration as a guaranteed completion date.',['Durations are estimates.','A critical activity taking longer can delay the entire completion date.','Managers need monitoring and contingency rather than assuming the calculated date is certain.','The network assumes resources can support parallel activities.','Shared staff or equipment may make the displayed overlap impossible.','Resource constraints must be checked before adopting the theoretical minimum schedule.'])]
    add(5,'bm96-critical-path','5.6','ahl',f'{name}: critical path and float','HL critical path analysis toolkit',f'{name} plans {project}. The activity-on-arrow network is supplied; arrows run from the lower-numbered event to the higher-numbered event. Durations are in days. An event occurs only after every incoming activity finishes. Activities may run in parallel, resources are unconstrained for the calculation, and the project starts at time 0. No drawing is required.',items,kind='data-response',svg=svgwrap(body,780,370),solution_svg=svgwrap(sol,780,370),check=f'PathACE={left+e};BDE={right+e};critical={critical};finish={finish};EST={est};LFT={lft};{short}totalfloat={slack},freefloat0. Drawing not demanded.')
ef(5,'bm97-contingency','5.7','ahl','Crisis and contingency planning','contingency planning','Preparing in advance for possible disruptive events / by identifying risks, responsibilities and fallback actions before a crisis occurs.','funding the proposed contingency plan',[
('Eza Meals','Eza supplies chilled meals. A backup generator and alternate cold store would cost USD 18000 yearly. Power failures are infrequent but a long outage could spoil meals and interrupt vulnerable customers’ deliveries.','Prepared backup can reduce disruption and safety risk;Eza can protect food and deliveries during an outage','The plan uses scarce funds even when no outage occurs;USD 18000 cannot also be spent on routine service improvements','Fund proportionate backup after risk assessment and test responsibilities; compare shared storage options rather than treating all safeguards as equally necessary.','The probability and financial or human impact of a long outage are uncertain.'),
('Fza Data','Fza proposes an off-site backup and incident drills. Managers want to announce the plan as complete before testing recovery; clients require rapid restoration of critical records.','Preparation can reduce recovery time;tested backups and assigned roles help restore client data','Untested plans can create false confidence;backup existence does not prove records can be restored quickly','Fund and test the recovery process with clear recovery targets before making claims; retain honest client communication.','Actual restoration time and backup integrity are unverified.'),
('Gza Events','Gza proposes a weather contingency venue for outdoor festivals. The reserved venue has limited capacity and requires a non-refundable deposit before ticket sales are known.','Advance reservation can preserve continuity;some activities can continue instead of complete cancellation','Capacity and sunk reservation cost limit the benefit;the backup cannot accommodate all advertised ticket holders','Match ticket limits and communication to realistic fallback capacity, and compare staged or insured alternatives.','Weather likelihood, ticket demand and the refund obligation are unknown.'),
('Hza Tools','Hza proposes dual sourcing for a critical component. The second supplier costs more and needs qualification tests; current managers rely on rapid reaction if the sole supplier fails.','A qualified alternative reduces dependence;Hza can restore production more quickly after supply failure','Readiness has an ongoing cost;testing and smaller split orders may reduce purchasing economies','Qualify a proportionate second source and define activation triggers; do not assume an untested supplier is a usable contingency.','The main supplier’s disruption risk and full qualification cost are not specified.')])
ef(5,'bm98-rd-intellectual-property','5.8','ahl','R&D and intellectual property','research and development','Systematic investigation and development of ideas, products or processes / to create or improve commercially or socially useful solutions.','pursuing the proposed innovation and protection strategy',[
('Iza Filters','Iza develops a novel filter cartridge. Patent filing is costly and would disclose the invention; a competitor may independently develop a similar design. Tests are promising but long-term reliability is unproven.','Patent protection can support returns on R&D;exclusive rights may help Iza recover development cost if the invention qualifies','Disclosure and expense create trade-offs;filing does not establish customer demand or guarantee long-term reliability','Continue staged testing and obtain appropriate patent advice before disclosure; choose protection based on enforceability and commercial value.','Patent eligibility, enforcement cost and long-term test results are unknown.'),
('Jza Games','Jza develops a distinctive educational game. It proposes copyright protection for original artwork and a trademark for its name, but managers assume this prevents every rival from using a similar teaching concept.','Appropriate rights can protect distinctive assets;artwork and brand identity may support Jza’s differentiation','The assumed scope is too broad;copyright and trademark do not automatically monopolize an underlying general teaching idea','Protect the original expression and brand while investing in quality and customer relationships; do not base the entire strategy on excluding all similar concepts.','The originality of assets and likely competitor responses are not established.'),
('Kza Mobility','Kza develops a cheaper modular wheelchair to meet unmet needs. A disruptive design could reach new users, but limited funds mean it cannot test every configuration before launch.','Innovation can address underserved demand;modularity may improve access and repairability for new users','Insufficient testing creates serious quality and trust risk;unverified configurations may fail in use','Stage the launch around validated configurations and user testing; seek partners for further R&D rather than offering unsafe untested options.','The configuration-specific reliability evidence and users’ affordability constraints are incomplete.'),
('Lza Materials','Lza proposes incremental improvements to a recycled panel rather than a wholly new material. The improvement reduces weight slightly but customers mainly complain about moisture performance.','Incremental R&D can use established capability;small changes may be quicker and less costly to develop','The improvement may not address the unmet need;lower weight does little if moisture failure drives rejection','Refocus testing on customer performance requirements and adopt the weight change only if it adds valued benefit at acceptable cost.','The relative importance of moisture resistance and weight has not been measured.')])
ef(5,'bm99-mis-ethics','5.9','ahl','Management information systems and ethics','management information system','An organized system for collecting, processing and communicating information / to support the management and decision-making needs of an organization.','introducing the proposed information system',[
('Mza Freight','Mza proposes AI route planning using drivers’ location and delivery histories. Managers expect fewer delays, but data can misclassify necessary breaks as unproductive time.','Analytics can improve routing decisions;historical journeys may reveal avoidable congestion and empty travel','Digital monitoring can create unfair pressure;misclassified breaks may damage motivation and safety','Pilot with human review, transparent data rules and protected breaks; evaluate route reliability and employee effects together.','Prediction accuracy, data quality and the effect on actual workloads are unknown.'),
('Nza Shops','Nza proposes a cloud-based loyalty database and personalized offers. Some customers share accounts, and the vendor’s default settings retain detailed histories indefinitely.','Data mining can improve offer relevance;Nza can identify patterns in purchases across stores','Privacy and accuracy risks arise;shared accounts can misidentify preferences and indefinite retention increases exposure','Use proportionate consent, retention limits and secure access, with an option to shop without profiling; test whether personalization adds value.','Customer acceptance, vendor safeguards and incremental sales benefit are unmeasured.'),
('Oza Farms','Oza proposes internet-connected irrigation sensors. A cloud service would automate watering, but rural connectivity is intermittent and the supplier controls the data format.','Connected data can improve resource decisions;soil readings may reduce unnecessary watering','Reliance on connectivity and one supplier creates operational risk;lost access or incompatible data could disrupt irrigation','Use local fallback controls and portable data arrangements before automation; verify reliability under actual rural conditions.','Connectivity failure rates, water savings and switching costs are not known.'),
('Pza Training','Pza proposes virtual-reality safety training using AI feedback. It can simulate hazardous situations, but staff have different accessibility needs and the model sometimes gives unexplained scores.','Simulation can provide safe repeatable practice;staff can experience rare hazards without creating real danger','Opaque or inaccessible scoring can undermine fairness;some trainees may be penalized by interface difficulty rather than unsafe knowledge','Validate feedback with qualified trainers and accessible alternatives; use the system to support rather than replace accountable assessment.','The validity of AI scores, accessibility testing and transfer to real performance are unknown.')])
for name,change,drives,restrains,proposal in [
('Qza Devices','introducing cellular manufacturing',[('Less movement',4),('Faster response',3)],[('Retraining fear',4),('Disruption',2)],'protected retraining time'),
('Rza Meals','changing to reusable containers',[('Less waste',5),('Customer interest',3)],[('Washing cost',4),('Return uncertainty',3)],'a small return-and-wash trial'),
('Sza Tools','introducing a shared quality system',[('Fewer defects',4),('Common information',4)],[('Blame concerns',5),('Training time',2)],'a no-blame pilot and funded training'),
('Tza Wear','moving to modular batches',[('More variety',3),('Lower changeover cost',4)],[('Skill gaps',4),('Supplier complexity',4)],'training and a limited component range')]:
    dsum=sum(x[1] for x in drives);rsum=sum(x[1] for x in restrains)
    table='\n\n| Driving force | Score | Restraining force | Score |\n|---|---:|---|---:|\n'+'\n'.join(f'| {drives[j][0]} | {drives[j][1]} | {restrains[j][0]} | {restrains[j][1]} |' for j in range(2))+'\n\n'
    items=[calc('a','Calculate the total driving score and total restraining score.',f'Driving={drives[0][1]}+{drives[1][1]}; restraining={restrains[0][1]}+{restrains[1][1]}.',f'Driving={dsum}; restraining={rsum}.'),explain('b',f'Explain how {proposal} could reduce resistance to this operational change.',['Addressing a restraining force can improve acceptance without simply increasing pressure.',f'The intervention ({proposal}) directly addresses the stated concerns about capability, cost or implementation.','It can give employees practical evidence that the operational change is manageable.','Managers should check whether the intervention actually reduces the relevant concern rather than assume a lower score.']),explain('c','Explain two limitations of using only the force-field totals to approve the change.',['Scores are subjective judgments.','Different employees may assign different importance to the same force.','The apparent numerical balance is not an objective probability of success.','Adding scores assumes forces can be compared and offset directly.','A critical safety or feasibility constraint cannot always be cancelled by several small benefits.','Management must investigate the underlying issues rather than approve a change solely from the higher total.'])]
    add(5,'bm100-force-field','5.3','ahl',f'{name}: force-field analysis','HL force-field toolkit',f'{name} considers {change}. A management workshop assigns scores from 1 (weak) to 5 (strong). The scores are judgments, not measured probabilities.'+table+f'Management proposes {proposal} before implementation. Show workings for totals and apply the analysis to the case.',items,kind='data-response',check=f'Drivingtotal={dsum};restrainingtotal={rsum}; scores not treated as statisticaldata, guaranteeddecision or additiveprobabilities.',scope='5.3 operational change integrated with explicitly HL-only force-field toolkit.')
